# Line endings for generated output
#
# The build scripts in tools/ write their output with LF line endings.
# This clone has core.autocrlf=true, which tells git the working tree
# should hold CRLF. With no .gitattributes, git therefore treats every
# freshly generated page as "modified" even when the bytes are identical
# to the committed blob, and prints a "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# warning for each one.
#
# That broke a check the audit runs depend on: every quality pass
# re-runs the six build scripts and treats a clean "git status" as proof
# that the committed pages still match what the generators produce. With
# 89 false positives in the way, real drift could not be told from noise.
#
# Declaring the generated directories as LF makes git stop trying to
# convert them, so the status check means something again. The rest of
# the repo is left alone: at the time of writing 47 files outside these
# directories hold CRLF and are untouched by this rule.
#
# Added 2026-08-06 during the quality pass on worklist item 3.1.

modules/service/pages/** text eol=lf
modules/switch/pages/**  text eol=lf
modules/branch/pages/**  text eol=lf

# The audit artefacts, added 2026-08-14 during the quality pass on item 3.5.
#
# The same fault as above, in the folder the audit trail itself lives in, and
# it was corrupting the very check the note above exists to protect.
#
# tools/check-live-hours.js ends every run with
# fs.writeFileSync(outFile, JSON.stringify(report, null, 2)), and
# JSON.stringify writes LF. With core.autocrlf=true and no rule for this
# folder, git checks the file out with CRLF (measured: 427 carriage returns)
# and the checker immediately rewrites it with LF, so git reports it modified
# on every run. The content is never modified: measured on the run that added
# this line, the working tree file and the index blob had the same sha and the
# same 27369 bytes, and git diff exited 0, while git status still printed " M".
#
# That matters because every quality pass re-runs the six build scripts and
# reads a clean git status as proof the committed pages still match what the
# generators produce. A pass also runs the checker suite, so that phantom sat
# in the status output and had to be investigated and dismissed by hand before
# the real result could be read. One file is a nuisance; the point of the note
# above is that noise and real drift stop being distinguishable once there is
# enough of it.
#
# Safe to declare: all 97 tracked files under audits/ already hold LF in the
# index, checked one by one before this was added, so nothing renormalises and
# no committed content changes.

audits/** text eol=lf
